# Build context for Dev/Warewolf.Execution.QueueProcessor/Dockerfile is the REPO ROOT, because the
# image builds from source and the worker links shared files from sibling projects (COPY Dev/ Dev/).
# `az acr build` tars this whole context and uploads it, so without these exclusions it:
#   * FAILS on '.vs' — Visual Studio holds the FileContentIndex *.vsidx files open, and the tar
#     step dies with "[Errno 13] Permission denied" (this blocked three Phase C runs)
#   * uploads every bin/ and obj/ across ~133 projects, which is gigabytes per deploy
#   * risks stale host build output leaking into the image; the Dockerfile restores and builds
#     inside the container, so host artefacts are never wanted
#
# TWO SYNTAX RULES, both learned the hard way from `az acr build --debug`, which logs
# ".dockerignore: rule '<p>' matches '<path>'" or ".dockerignore: no rule for '<path>'":
#
#  1. NO TRAILING SLASH. The CLI tests context-relative paths with no trailing separator
#     ('Dev/.vs'), so a pattern written '.vs/' matches nothing at all.
#  2. LIST BOTH THE BARE AND '**/' FORM. A bare name is anchored at the context root, and the
#     CLI's matcher does not treat '**/' as also matching depth zero. This repo has FOUR .vs
#     directories - root, Dev, and two under ClientExamples - so both forms are required.
#
# Verify any change with:
#   az acr build --registry <acr> --image probe:t --file <dockerfile> <context> --no-push --debug
# and confirm the offending paths report "rule ... matches", not "no rule for".
.vs
**/.vs
.vscode
**/.vscode
bin
**/bin
obj
**/obj
TestResults
**/TestResults
node_modules
**/node_modules
packages
**/packages
.github
*.user
*.suo
*.log

# Publish output: the image is built from source, not from a host publish. Staged deployment inputs
# under these folders can also contain credentials, so they must never enter a layer.
publish
**/publish
Settings.local
**/Settings.local
